# 🐀 Rat devtools

Joel approved adopting Foldkit's devtools MCP and Oxlint plugin ideas on 2026-09-23 and asked for a devtools designed from rat-stack's own perspective. This note is the proposal. It links [layer-startups](./layer-startups.svx) for the build order and [same-version-repos](../resources/same-version-repos.svx) for the peer study.

Sources read: [foldkit/foldkit](https://github.com/foldkit/foldkit) at `95fed7f` (`packages/devtools-mcp`, `packages/devtools`, `packages/oxlint-plugin-foldkit`), and Rubicon's badass-devtools (ported from course-builder).

## What Foldkit gets right

Foldkit's devtools MCP (`@foldkit/devtools-mcp` 0.22, about 1,200 lines) attaches an agent to a running app. A browser bridge streams the Runtime's history over Vite's HMR socket to a relay, and a stdio MCP server exposes fifteen tools. Three ideas carry the design:

1. **Dispatch through the Schema.** The agent asks for the Message Schema as JSON Schema, then dispatches Messages that the Runtime decodes before `update` sees them. A bad payload fails with a clean error. The agent never guesses at shapes and never reads source to learn them.
2. **History as a queryable log.** Every Message is an entry with its Commands, changed paths, and timestamp. Agents count by tag, filter by changed path, tail with `from_end`, and diff two indices server-side instead of pulling whole snapshots.
3. **Token-bounded reads.** Reads take a `root.`-anchored dot path and summarize large values (`{ _summary: 'array', length, sample }`) unless `expand: true`. Paths copied from one tool's output work in the next.

Badass-devtools is the human half: a draggable logo, a ⌘K palette, a test-account toggle, and user and env tabs. It is admin-only and has no agent surface.

## The rat-stack mapping

Foldkit has one loop: Message in, Model out. Rat-stack has three places where state moves, and each maps to Foldkit's nouns.

| Foldkit | Rat-stack | Where it lives |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Message Schema | A contract's input, output, and failure Schemas | `defineContract`, `@rat-stack/core/contracts` |
| Dispatch a Message | Call a capability by name, input decoded by its contract | `implement`; approval still gates |
| Message history | Capability call log: name, input, outcome, failure tag, duration | New `CallLog` in `packages/devtools` |
| Model | Server: none, the host is authoritative. Machines: XState snapshots. Client: the AtomRpc replica | `@xstate/effect` `inspect`, AtomRpc registry |
| Keyframes and replay | Replay a recorded call and diff the outcomes; read a machine snapshot at an index | Devtools capabilities |
| Durable history | `RunLog`, per person, in the database | `packages/database` |

Server state has no single Model to time-travel, and it should not grow one: the Durable Object or database is authoritative. So rat devtools replays calls instead of rewinding state. Machines and the client replica do have state over time, and those get Foldkit-style snapshots in later slices.

The call log is ephemeral and per process, for debugging. `RunLog` is durable and per person, for the product. They stay separate; devtools can read `RunLog` later.

## Devtools are capabilities

The devtools tools are themselves contracts and capabilities in `packages/devtools`. That is the rat-stack move: one contract, every surface. The same `rat_list_calls` contract becomes an MCP tool through `toToolkit`, a CLI command through `toCommand`, and an RPC for the 🐀 overlay through `toRpcGroup`. Nothing in devtools bypasses the projection layer, and the boundary rules apply to it like any other cartridge.

Recording uses an ambient observer: `@rat-stack/capability/call-watch` exports `CallWatch`, a `Context.Reference` whose default passes the handler through. `implement` reads it once per call and runs the approval-gated handler through its `around` function. Devtools provides the recording implementation from `record(options)` through `devtoolsLayer()`, so every projection records without receiving a wrapped capability list. `aroundHandlers` remains a separate explicit wrapper for policy over one list.

## MCP tool list

Prefix `rat_`. Each tool accepts an optional `runtime_id` once more than one runtime can attach.

| Tool | Does | Slice |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `rat_list_contracts` | Variant index: name, description, approval, annotations for every capability in the runtime | 1 |
| `rat_describe_contract` | Input, output, and failure JSON Schema for one contract, derived the way `toToolkit` derives it | 1 |
| `rat_list_calls` | Call history, filtered by capability and outcome, with `limit`, `since_index`, and `from_end` | 1 |
| `rat_count_calls` | Counts by capability and outcome, no payloads. The cheap first call | 1 |
| `rat_get_call` | One entry, with `path` and `expand` over its input and output | 1 |
| `rat_call` | Call a capability by name. Input is decoded by its contract; approval-gated capabilities stay gated | 1 |
| `rat_replay_call` | Re-run a recorded call's input, record the result, and return a path diff of the two outputs | 1 |
| `rat_diff_calls` | Path-level diff of two entries' inputs and outputs | 1 |
| `rat_list_runtimes` | Every attached runtime: CLI `serve`, Worker under `alchemy dev`, browser tabs | 2 |
| `rat_list_machines`, `rat_get_machine`, `rat_list_transitions` | Live `@xstate/effect` actors: state value, event history, context by path | 2 |
| `rat_list_atoms`, `rat_get_atom` | The browser's AtomRpc replica, by atom key and path | 3 |
| `rat_list_runs` | A person's durable `RunLog` entries | 3 |

Reads use Foldkit's path alphabet and summary shape (`root.output.matches.0`, `expand`), so agents who know one devtools know the other. We write our own small implementation rather than copying Foldkit's.

## The 🐀 overlay

A later slice, once `apps/web` lands. It follows badass-devtools' shape (a draggable 🐀 trigger and a ⌘K palette) but reads everything through the devtools contracts over `toRpcGroup`, so the overlay and the MCP show the same truth. Tabs:

- Calls;
- Contracts, with a dispatch form generated from the input JSON Schema;
- Machines;
- Atoms;
- Session, with a test-person toggle like Rubicon's test athlete mode, once auth lands.

It is a feature plus a client, so the boundary rules fence it like any other.

## Where it runs

Foldkit's agent attaches to a running app, and so does ours. In slice 1 the app is the CLI:

- `rat serve --devtools` serves the HTTP API as today and adds MCP over HTTP at `/__rat/mcp`. The agent attaches with `claude mcp add --transport http rat-devtools http://127.0.0.1:3000/__rat/mcp`, then sees calls that browsers, curl, or tests make against the API.
- `rat mcp --devtools` adds the devtools tools beside the normal tools on stdio, for an agent inspecting its own calls.

Devtools listens on `127.0.0.1` only. AGENTS.md requires owner sign-off to expose `serve` or `mcp` beyond localhost, and `serve` today binds every interface through `createServer().listen(port)`. That is worth fixing separately.

In a Worker the log needs a home that outlives one isolate: a Durable Object, like the legacy MCP sessions. That is slice 2, under `alchemy dev` only.

## Lint rules: what translates

Foldkit ships 37 rules on [`effect-oxlint`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/effect-oxlint), each with real-oxlint fixture tests like ours.

**Adopt, rewritten for rat-stack:**

| Foldkit rule | Rat-stack rule | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `no-hand-rolled-command-struct` | `no-hand-rolled-surface`: no `Rpc.make`, `RpcGroup.make`, `HttpApiEndpoint`, `Tool.make`, or `Command.make` over domain work outside `packages/capability` and `apps/cli/src/command.ts` | The capability-only surface rule is prose today. The peer study's fifth recommendation asks for exactly this fence |
| `command-binding-matches-name` | `contract-binding-matches-name`: `const search = defineContract("search", …)` | Agents grep by name; a mismatched binding hides the contract |
| `no-module-level-mutable-state` | Same, for `apps/*/src/**` and `packages/*/src/**` | In a Worker, module state leaks across requests in one isolate. That is a real bug class, not style |
| `acquire-release-constructs-in-acquire-body` | Same | Hyperdrive and pg pools must be built inside the acquire, per invocation. The auth lane is about to write that code |
| `no-nonportable-server-globals` | `no-browser-globals-on-server`: no `window` or `document` outside `client/` and `features/` | The mirror of our browser boundary |

**Already covered:**

| Foldkit rule | Covered by |
| --- | --- |
| `no-impure-call-at-decision-time` | Effect diagnostics `globalDate`, `globalRandom`, and `globalTimers` at `error` |
| `no-switch-on-message-tag` | `anti-slop-effect/prefer-effect-match` and `no-manual-tag-comparison` |
| `prefer-callable-message-constructor` | `anti-slop-effect/no-manual-tagged-construction` |
| `no-array-index-view-keys` | Oxlint's React rules, once `apps/web` enables them |
| `require-rel-for-external-link` | Oxlint's React rules, once `apps/web` enables them |

**Leave with Foldkit.** The Submodel, `Got*Message`, `keyed`, `lazy`, `Mount`, `OnMount`, `modifyFields`, route-query, and Stream `preventDefault` rules only mean something in a Foldkit app. If a Foldkit bin lands, it ships with `@foldkit/oxlint-plugin` itself, not our port.

## Foldkit as a UI cartridge

Foldkit fits the thesis better than any React stack: the Model is a Schema, side effects are declared Commands, failures arrive as data, and it ships its own lint and agent tooling. The contract split makes it cheap to try, because a Foldkit Command can call the same `toRpcGroup` client that `apps/web` uses.

Against it as the default: it replaces React, so the React ecosystem is out except through embedding. It is pre-1.0 with minor-version breaks. It pins `effect` and `@effect/platform-browser` to exactly `4.0.0-rc.116` as peers, one release behind us, so every Effect bump waits on theirs.

Decision (Joel, 2026-09-23): no Foldkit bin. Effect all the way down into the view is not useful right now. It should stay possible, and the contract split keeps it possible: any UI that can call `toRpcGroup` can sit on the same backend. We take Foldkit's devtools and lint ideas, not its framework.

## First slice

`packages/devtools`, server side, no browser:

- `CallLog` service, a bounded in-memory ring buffer (500 entries by default), with `record(options)` as the `CallWatch` layer constructor and `devtoolsLayer()` composing it with the logs;
- the slice 1 contracts and capabilities from the tool table, with path and summary reads;
- `--devtools` on `rat serve` (MCP over HTTP at `/__rat/mcp`, `127.0.0.1` only) and on `rat mcp` (stdio);
- tests in memory: record, count, filter, `from_end`, `rat_call` with a bad payload, approval still denies, replay with a diff, and summarization.

Cartridge test: one package, one flag branch in `apps/cli/src/surfaces.ts`. Delete both and the gate passes.

## Decisions

Joel, 2026-09-23: build the devtools MCP and the lint rules. Order: the server-side devtools slice first, then the five lint rules. No Foldkit bin. Tool prefix `rat_`.

## Slice 1 status

Built on `worker/foldkit-explore`, base `8dffe1f`:

- `packages/devtools`: `CallLog`, `record`, `devtools`, and the eight slice 1 contracts in a browser-safe `./contracts` subpath.
- `packages/capability` gained `aroundHandlers`, an explicit wrapper over one capability list, and `invokerFor`, code mode's dispatch path, now exported so `rat_call` reuses it instead of copying it.
- `rat serve --devtools` and `rat mcp --devtools [--code-mode]`.
- Verified live: a REST call to `serve --devtools` shows up in `rat_list_calls` over MCP HTTP, and the listener is on `127.0.0.1` only.

A `Schema.Struct({})` contract input originally projected to `{"not":{"type":"null"}}`, which MCP rejected because a tool's `inputSchema` needs `"type": "object"`. The toolkit projection now advertises the empty object shape and leaves decoding to the contract schema; integration tests cover registration, listing, and calling. OpenAPI and the code-mode catalog use the same empty-input schema normalization.

## Slice 2 status

The five lint rules from the translation table landed on the same branch, each with real-oxlint fixture tests (`packages/core/test/patterns-rule.test.ts` and new cases in `boundaries-rule.test.ts`). Breaking a rule on purpose fails its test.

- `rat-stack-boundaries/no-hand-rolled-surface`
- `rat-stack-boundaries/no-browser-globals-on-server`
- `rat-stack-patterns/contract-binding-matches-name`
- `rat-stack-patterns/no-module-level-mutable-state` (skips `.svelte`, where `let` in `<script>` is per-instance)
- `rat-stack-patterns/acquire-release-constructs-in-acquire-body`

All five start at zero findings. The only renames were code mode's `contract` to `executeContract` and the devtools contracts to `ratListCalls` and friends.

## Slice 3 status: machines

- `@rat-stack/capability/actor-watch`: `ActorWatch`, a `Context.Reference` whose default does nothing, and `watchActor(machine, actor)`. `WatchableActor` is structural (`sessionId`, `getSnapshot`, `inspect`), so the capability package needs no XState import. Core calls it once, in `runInspectMachine`.
- The devtools recorder provides a real watcher to every recorded call. It subscribes to `actor.inspect` synchronously, so fast transitions are not missed. It parses each XState inspection event with a Schema, learns child actor names from every snapshot's `children`, and drains its queue when the machine's scope closes, so final transitions are not lost. Pulling devtools out leaves the no-op in place.
- `ActorLog` shares the ring buffer with `CallLog`. New tools: `rat_list_actors`, `rat_list_transitions`, `rat_get_actor`.
- Proved end to end: a REST `inspectFile` call on `serve --devtools` records `inspectMachine` going `reading` to `inspected`, and its `readStats` child. Deleting the `watchActor` line fails that test.
- `rat-stack-patterns/watch-effect-actors` fails lint when runtime source calls `createEffectActor` without `watchActor`.

## Queued

Joel, 2026-09-23: queue all three; Rubicon's badass-devtools "are broken and suck", so nothing is copied from them.

1. **Client atoms, slice 4.** Built; see below. Was blocked on `apps/web`. A browser bridge reports the AtomRpc registry (atom keys, values by path, refresh history) to the same devtools process, so `rat_list_atoms` and `rat_get_atom` read the client replica. The open design question is transport: the dev server's `/__rat` route, or the `/rpc` route the web lane builds.
2. **🐀 overlay, slice 5.** Built; see below. Was blocked on `apps/web`. A feature plus a client in `apps/web`, reading only the devtools contracts through `toRpcGroup`: Calls, Contracts with a dispatch form generated from JSON Schema, Machines, Atoms. It replaces badass-devtools in vendoring apps instead of sitting beside it.
3. **Session and test person, slice 6.** Built; see below. The Session tab moves into slice 5.

## Slice 6 status: run as a test person

Built on `3269e50` after `packages/auth` landed.

- Devtools stays independent of auth. `devtools(capabilities, { runAs })` takes a generic `RunAs` (`label`, `schema`, `tag`). The recorder reads the identity service on every call and stores it as `as` on the call entry. `rat_call` accepts `as`, decodes it with `runAs.schema`, and provides it to the dispatched call. `rat_replay_call` replays as the recorded identity. An `as` that does not decode is refused, not guessed.
- `@rat-stack/auth/devtools` supplies the auth half:
  - `runAsPerson` (`CurrentPerson` plus `PersonIdSchema`);
  - `rat_test_person`, which signs a test person in (creating them if needed) and returns the person id and session cookie. The email is always `<name>@rat.test` with a fixed development password, so it cannot reach a real account;
  - `testPersonLayer(name)`, which gives a development composition a default `CurrentPerson`.
- Why a default is needed: a capability that requires `CurrentPerson` puts it in the devtools toolkit's requirements. A dev server provides `testPersonLayer("dev")` once, and `as` overrides it per call.
- `rat_test_person` uses Better Auth's raw `auth.auth` handle, because Alchemy's API proxy drops the `returnHeaders` typing on `signUpEmail`.
- Integration test: `packages/auth/test/devtools.test.ts`, on Better Auth's memory database. Making dispatch ignore `as` fails two of its tests.
- Not wired into `apps/cli` yet: no CLI capability needs a person, and the CLI has no `Auth`. The web composition adds `ratTestPerson` to its devtools list and passes `{ runAs: runAsPerson }`.

## Production guardrail

The owner's condition on 2026-09-23: test people and devtools must never be in a deployed composition. `rat_test_person` creates accounts with a fixed password, and `rat_call` runs as a default identity, bypassing per-request auth.

`rat-stack-boundaries/no-devtools-in-production` enforces this. `@rat-stack/devtools` and `@rat-stack/auth/devtools` may be imported only from:

- `packages/devtools`
- `packages/auth`
- `apps/cli`
- test folders
- `apps/*/src/dev/**`

Nothing outside an app's `dev/` folder may import from it. The dev composition is therefore its own entry: the web app's Website resource points `main` at `src/dev/…` under `alchemy dev` and at the production entry otherwise, by path string, never by import. Slices 4 and 5 put the devtools wiring there: `ratTestPerson` in the list, `{ runAs: runAsPerson }`, and `testPersonLayer("dev")` as the default person.

## Slices 4 and 5 status: the web dev loop, the overlay, and atoms

Built on `a68fb4b` after `apps/web` landed.

- **The dev loop came first, in its own commit.** `vite dev` returned 500 on every page, because `routes/rpc.ts` imported `env` from `cloudflare:workers`. The web lane's "alchemy dev smoke passed" was true: it had used a temporary Stack, `apps/web/stack-smoke.tmp.ts`, which it deleted afterward, so nothing on main could reproduce it. `/rpc` now takes its backend from the `#backend` import in `apps/web/package.json`. The `development` condition serves the content capabilities in process from `src/dev/`, and the default forwards to the `RpcBackend` binding.
- **Devtools layer on top.** `src/dev/devtools/routes.ts` runs the content capabilities plus `rat_test_person` under `devtools(…, { runAs: runAsPerson })`. It serves recorded `/rpc`, `/__rat/rpc` for the overlay, and `/__rat/mcp` for agents, with memory Better Auth and `testPersonLayer("dev")`. Removing it is one line in `src/dev/backend.ts`.
- **The overlay.** A 🐀 button and ⌘K open four tabs:
  - Calls, the recent log;
  - Contracts, with the input JSON Schema, a JSON editor, and Run, optionally as the selected person;
  - Machines;
  - Session, which signs in a test person and uses them for dispatch.

  It is a client and a feature under `src/dev/`, with its own `RegistryProvider`, so its atoms never mix with the app's. The boundary rules treat `apps/*/src/dev/{client,features}` as browser code, and `@rat-stack/devtools/contracts` is browser-safe.
- **Atoms.** The overlay mounts a reporter atom on the app's registry. Every 1.5 seconds, and only when something changed, it sends `rat_report_atoms` with each keyed atom (AtomRpc queries) or labeled atom (`Atom.withLabel`): the key, the state, and the value as JSON. Anonymous runtime atoms are skipped, so labeling an atom opts it in. Agents read the result with `rat_list_atoms` and `rat_get_atom`.
- **Verified in a real browser:**
  - a search shows up in the Calls tab, recorded as the dev person;
  - signing in `ada` from Session works;
  - Contracts runs `search` as `ada`, and the log shows her person id;
  - `rat_get_atom` over `/__rat/rpc` returns the page's `AtomRpc:search:capability` result.
- **One bug found and fixed on the way.** The selected person was a plain `Atom.make` and reset to `null` when the Session tab unmounted. It is now `Atom.keepAlive`.
- **Guardrail test.** `apps/web/test/production-bundle.test.ts` builds with source maps. It asserts no module from `src/dev/`, `packages/devtools`, or `packages/auth/src/devtools`, and no `rat_call`, `rat_test_person`, or test-password string. A `NODE_ENV=development` control build must contain all of them.
- **Known noise.** Better Auth logs "User not found" once per dev-server start, before `rat_test_person` falls back to sign-up.

## Call recording: ambient observer

Decided with the layer-startups and drovr lanes on 2026-09-24:

- `implement` reads `CallWatch` once for every capability call and passes the approval-gated handler through `around`, so approval denials are observable.
- `record(options)` is the direct `CallWatch` layer constructor. `devtoolsLayer(capacity, options)` provides it with `CallLog` and `ActorLog` and includes the logs in its output.
- `devtools(capabilities)` returns the original capability tuple as `recorded`; it does not wrap handlers or lists. The `CallWatch` layer is provided at the dev composition boundary, so MCP, code mode, and RPC observe the same calls.
- `aroundHandlers` remains explicit per-list control. `writeGate` can serialize and audit approval-gated writes with it. `implement` reads `CallWatch` inside the wrapped handler, so the gate runs outside the recorder.
- The production default is a no-op reference. Production projections pay one reference read per call and need no devtools dependency.

## Closed out, 2026-09-23

Everything Joel queued has shipped on main:

- the call log and contract dispatch (slice 1);
- the five Foldkit lint rules (slice 2);
- machines (slice 3);
- browser atoms (slice 4);
- the 🐀 overlay (slice 5);
- run as a test person (slice 6);
- the production guardrail;
- the web dev loop.

Main has since deployed `Website` and `RpcBackend` to prod, where `/__rat/mcp` returns 404, and moved `pnpm dev` to portless at `http://rat-stack.localhost:1355`. The last pass also added `baseURL` for the dev Better Auth, read from `PORTLESS_URL` (declared in `.env.schema`). It silences the "Base URL could not be determined" warning on every `pnpm dev`. Without portless, for example the `vite dev` tests, the URL is unset and the warning returns, which is harmless.

`rat serve` binding to `127.0.0.1` also landed on main, in `91a6dcc`.

Not built, on purpose, until a consumer needs them:

- **`rat_list_runtimes`.** Each process has one devtools runtime today, so there is nothing to choose between.
- **`rat_list_runs`.** Nothing writes to `RunLog` yet. Build it beside the first capability that records runs, as a dev tool in `packages/database` like `rat_test_person` in auth, so devtools does not import database.
- **A Worker-side call log in a Durable Object.** The dev loop runs in Node under `vite dev`. Revisit if `alchemy dev` becomes the dev loop.

Gotcha: portless can choose an app port that another unregistered dev server already holds (seen with a pi-notes `vite dev`). `PORTLESS_APP_PORT=<port> pnpm dev` pins one.

Checks for future slices: the gate plus `bash scripts/acceptance-cold-clone.sh`.

